item.content:encoded:
You have a new prompt, model, or orchestration policy. Offline tests look good, but the change will see vocabulary, timing, and context combinations that your fixture set never captured.So you shadow-test it: send production traffic to both the current agent and the candidate, compare their decisions, and keep the current agent in control.Then the candidate calls  too.Now one user request has produced two real messages. The “safe” experiment has become a duplicate-action incident.This synthetic failure happens when shadowing is implemented as “run the whole application twice.” For agents that can change the world, shadow testing must duplicate observation and decision—not authority and effects.The Architecture Boundary That Makes Shadowing SafeAn action-taking agent contains at least four separable stages:input -> decision proposal -> policy/approval -> tool execution -> outcome
The shadow should stop after the proposal and policy simulation:                         +--> primary decision --> real policy --> execute
sanitized input/context -|
                         +--> shadow decision  --> dry policy  --> record only
This boundary is useful beyond experiments. It makes approvals testable, prevents a model response from directly becoming an external write, and gives you structured evidence to compare.Represent Intent Before ExecutionInstead of letting the model own an executable tool object, normalize its choice into a proposal:type ActionProposal = {
  action: string;
  schemaVersion: number;
  args: Record<string, unknown>;
  reasonCode: string;
  confidence?: {
    value: number;
    source: "model" | "calibrator";
  };
};

type PolicyDecision = {
  result: "allow" | "deny" | "require_approval";
  policyVersion: string;
  reasonCodes: string[];
};
Do not collect hidden chain-of-thought. A bounded reason code such as  or  is reviewable. If you store a short explanation, treat it as untrusted, redact it, and never require private reasoning from a model.The executor accepts only a proposal that has passed real policy:async function executeAuthorized(
  proposal: ActionProposal,
  decision: PolicyDecision,
  context: ExecutionContext,
) {
  if (context.mode !== "primary") {
    throw new Error("Effects are disabled outside primary mode");
  }
  if (decision.result !== "allow") {
    throw new Error(`Action is not authorized: ${decision.result}`);
  }

  return toolRegistry.execute(proposal, context);
}
That mode check is defense in depth. The stronger control is that the shadow environment never receives write-capable credentials.A  flag passed into a real tool is fragile. One tool may ignore it, a refactor may omit it, or a newly registered tool may default to execution.Build a distinct registry:type ToolDescriptor = {
  name: string;
  inputSchema: object;
  sideEffect: "read" | "write";
};

const shadowTools = realTools.map(({ name, inputSchema, sideEffect }) => ({
  name,
  inputSchema,
  sideEffect,
} satisfies ToolDescriptor));
The candidate sees the same tool names and schemas needed to make a decision, but the objects have no  method. If it proposes , the harness records the arguments and predicted policy result.At the infrastructure layer, use separate identities and network policy:no production write tokens;no direct access to message brokers used for effects;read-only or synthetic dependencies where possible;a separate telemetry namespace;hard spend and concurrency budgets; anda kill switch independent of model behavior.Application checks can fail. Missing credentials fail closed.Decide What Context the Shadow Is Allowed to SeeCopying an entire production prompt into an experiment can expose personal, contractual, or security-sensitive data. Define a context projection:type ShadowContext = {
  requestClass: string;
  locale: string;
  accountTier: string;
  recentEventCodes: string[];
  policyFacts: Record<string, boolean | number | string>;
};

function toShadowContext(input: LiveContext): ShadowContext {
  return {
    requestClass: input.requestClass,
    locale: input.locale,
    accountTier: input.accountTier,
    recentEventCodes: input.events.map(event => event.code).slice(-10),
    policyFacts: selectApprovedPolicyFacts(input),
  };
}
Run this projection through privacy and threat review. The candidate often needs less context than the primary runtime accumulated.Two good agents can explain the same decision differently. Text similarity therefore creates noisy promotion gates.Compare structured behavior:type DecisionDiff = {
  sameAction: boolean;
  changedArgumentPaths: string[];
  primaryPolicy: PolicyDecision["result"];
  shadowPolicy: PolicyDecision["result"];
  riskClass: "equivalent" | "benign_change" | "review" | "unsafe";
};
The highest-signal mismatches are usually:primary says “stay silent,” candidate proposes a write;primary requests approval, candidate predicts allow;both choose the same tool but differ on amount, recipient, or scope;candidate calls a forbidden or nonexistent tool;candidate exceeds latency, token, or tool-call budgets; orcandidate makes a user-facing claim unsupported by the proposed evidence.Google's Agents CLI evaluation guide reflects this broader view of agent quality: it separates tool-use quality, trajectory quality, task success, final-response quality, hallucination, grounding, and safety. A shadow score should likewise be a vector, not a single “similarity” percentage.Use Policy Simulation CarefullyThe shadow can run the current policy engine in a non-authoritative mode. That reveals whether the candidate's proposal  be allowed.const simulated = await policy.evaluate({
  proposal: shadowProposal,
  principal: redactedPrincipalFacts,
  mode: "simulate",
  policyVersion: productionPolicyVersion,
});
Simulation must not mint approval tokens, reserve inventory, mutate rate-limit counters, or write audit decisions into the authoritative ledger. If the policy system cannot evaluate without side effects, build a pure policy core and keep recording outside it.Also record the policy version. A candidate comparison is uninterpretable if primary and shadow were evaluated under different rules without saying so.It is easy to shadow only the simple requests that survive a convenient filter. The resulting dashboard says the candidate is safe because risky traffic never entered the experiment.Define inclusion explicitly:type ShadowEligibility = {
  eligible: boolean;
  reason:
    | "selected"
    | "privacy_excluded"
    | "unsupported_tool"
    | "budget_excluded"
    | "control_group";
};
Report both the evaluated population and exclusions. Stratify results by action type, risk level, language, account segment, workflow length, and whether the primary required recovery. Otherwise a large aggregate can hide the exact slice that matters.One primary output versus one candidate output does not tell you the candidate's variance. For a bounded sample, run multiple candidate trials with fixed configuration and preserve the random seed where the provider supports it.Do not mistake a seed for full determinism. Provider updates, retrieval changes, timestamps, tool data, and concurrency can still change behavior.Useful summaries include:action agreement distribution
forbidden-action proposal rate
approval-bypass proposal rate
argument-diff severity
p50/p95 decision latency
token and cost distribution
variance across repeated candidate runs
coverage and exclusion rate
Never invent thresholds because they sound mature. Set them from product risk and baseline behavior, then document the decision.Shadowing is one stage, not a release strategy by itself. schemas, tool allowlists, secret scanning, policy tests. sanitized, versioned fixtures with known expected behavior.Repeated model evaluation: measure variance and grader stability. real context projection, no effect authority.Internal or consented canary: narrow population, real effects, rollback controls. risk-aware expansion with outcome monitoring.Promotion should consider downstream outcomes, not only proposal agreement. The current agent may be consistently wrong. A candidate that disagrees could be an improvement, so route meaningful disagreements to labeled review rather than automatically treating the primary as truth.What a Shadow Record Should ContainA reviewable record needs enough context to reproduce the comparison without becoming a privacy archive:type ShadowRecord = {
  experimentId: string;
  requestFingerprint: string;
  cohort: string;
  primary: { proposal: ActionProposal; policy: PolicyDecision };
  candidate: { proposal: ActionProposal; policy: PolicyDecision };
  diff: DecisionDiff;
  primaryVersion: string;
  candidateVersion: string;
  policyVersion: string;
  evaluatedAt: string;
};
Store raw prompts and outputs only when authorized and necessary. Prefer schema-approved fields, stable fingerprints, and short retention. Observability should reduce uncertainty without expanding the blast radius of sensitive data.The Non-Negotiable InvariantI would encode this as an automated contract:For every shadow run:
  external_write_count == 0
  authoritative_approval_count == 0
  production_message_publish_count == 0
If the telemetry needed to verify those invariants is missing, the experiment should fail closed or pause. “We did not see a write” is weaker than “the shadow identity could not write.”The point of shadow testing is to learn from realistic inputs before granting realistic authority. Duplicate the decision surface. Keep the effect surface singular.

The smallest game worth architecting: an immutable board, testable rules, a view that just renders state, and the classic AI you can bolt on in 40 linesTic tac toe is the "hello world" of game development, which is exactly why it's worth doing properly. It's small enough to hold in your head, but it still has every piece a real game needs: state, rules, turns, an end condition, a UI that reflects state, and, if you want it, an opponent that thinks.One of my earliest App Store apps is , published in April 2020. Its listing is as short as the game: "Put away your pencil and paper", with one-player and two-player modes, so you can play against another person or against yourself.This article uses tic tac toe to show an architecture I'd use for any turn-based game: a  that owns all the rules, a  that only renders state and forwards taps,  for the rules, and a  as an optional extension. To be clear: the listing doesn't say the app has a computer opponent. The minimax section is the approach I'd add, not a description of the app. API names are checked against Apple's documentation.1. Why architecture matters even here.The quick version of tic tac toe puts nine  strings in a view and checks for a winner inside the button action. It works for an afternoon. Then you want an undo button, a computer player, a "new game" animation, a stats screen and tests, and every one of those features has to reach into the view.Instead, split it like this:Game (struct)      ← all rules: whose turn, legal moves, winner, draw
   ▲     │
   │     ▼
GameViewModel      ← owns a Game, applies moves, triggers AI, publishes state
   ▲     │
   │     ▼
GameView (SwiftUI) ← renders state, sends taps; no rules at all
The model is a plain struct with no UIKit, SwiftUI, or timers, which means you can test it, copy it (undo is free), and hand a copy to an AI to explore future moves without touching the real game.2. The Model: A board that can't be wrong.enum Player: String, CaseIterable {
    case x = "X", o = "O"
    var opponent: Player { self == .x ? .o : .x }
}

enum Outcome: Equatable {
    case inProgress
    case win(Player, line: [Int])
    case draw
}

struct Game: Equatable {
    private(set) var cells: [Player?] = Array(repeating: nil, count: 9)
    private(set) var current: Player = .x
    private(set) var moves: [Int] = []          // history, for undo and replays

    static let lines: [[Int]] = [
        [0, 1, 2], [3, 4, 5], [6, 7, 8],         // rows
        [0, 3, 6], [1, 4, 7], [2, 5, 8],         // columns
        [0, 4, 8], [2, 4, 6]                     // diagonals
    ]

    var outcome: Outcome {
        for line in Self.lines {
            if let p = cells[line[0]], cells[line[1]] == p, cells[line[2]] == p {
                return .win(p, line: line)
            }
        }
        return cells.allSatisfy { $0 != nil } ? .draw : .inProgress
    }

    var legalMoves: [Int] {
        guard outcome == .inProgress else { return [] }
        return cells.indices.filter { cells[$0] == nil }
    }

    /// Returns a new game with the move applied, or nil if the move is illegal.
    func playing(_ index: Int) -> Game? {
        guard legalMoves.contains(index) else { return nil }
        var next = self
        next.cells[index] = current
        next.moves.append(index)
        next.current = current.opponent
        return next
    }

    func undoing() -> Game {
        guard let last = moves.last else { return self }
        var previous = self
        previous.cells[last] = nil
        previous.moves.removeLast()
        previous.current = current.opponent
        return previous
    }
}
A few deliberate choices: Nothing outside the model can put an X in an occupied square or skip a turn. The only way to change the game is , and it validates. returns a new value instead of mutating. That makes the AI trivial, because it explores copies, and it makes "what if?" previews free.Eight hard-coded lines beat a clever loop. For a 3×3 board, a literal table is the most readable rules engine possible. (For an N×N variant, you'd generate the lines instead.)Because the model is pure, the tests read like the rules of the game:import XCTest

final class GameTests: XCTestCase {

    private func play(_ moves: [Int]) -> Game {
        moves.reduce(Game()) { game, move in
            guard let next = game.playing(move) else {
                XCTFail("Illegal move \(move)"); return game
            }
            return next
        }
    }

    func testRowWin() {
        // X: 0, 1, 2   O: 3, 4
        XCTAssertEqual(play([0, 3, 1, 4, 2]).outcome, .win(.x, line: [0, 1, 2]))
    }

    func testDiagonalWinForO() {
        // X: 0, 1, 5   O: 2, 4, 6
        XCTAssertEqual(play([0, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6]).outcome, .win(.o, line: [2, 4, 6]))
    }

    func testDraw() {
        // X O X / X O O / O X X
        XCTAssertEqual(play([0, 1, 2, 4, 3, 5, 7, 6, 8]).outcome, .draw)
    }

    func testCannotPlayOccupiedSquareOrAfterWin() {
        let game = play([0, 3, 1, 4, 2])          // X has won
        XCTAssertNil(Game().playing(0)?.playing(0))
        XCTAssertNil(game.playing(8))
    }

    func testUndoRestoresTurn() {
        let game = play([4]).undoing()
        XCTAssertEqual(game, Game())
    }
}
These run in milliseconds, with no simulator UI. When you later add an AI or a new mode, they're your safety net.4. The View: Render state, forward taps.The view model holds the game and the mode. The view is a grid:import SwiftUI

@MainActor
final class GameViewModel: ObservableObject {
    enum Mode { case twoPlayer, onePlayer(computer: Player) }

    @Published private(set) var game = Game()
    @Published var mode: Mode = .twoPlayer
    @Published private(set) var isThinking = false

    func tap(_ index: Int) {
        if case .onePlayer(let computer) = mode, game.current == computer { return }
        guard let next = game.playing(index) else { return }
        game = next
        computerMoveIfNeeded()
    }

    func undo() { game = game.undoing() }
    func reset() { game = Game(); computerMoveIfNeeded() }

    private func computerMoveIfNeeded() {
        guard case .onePlayer(let computer) = mode,
              game.current == computer, game.outcome == .inProgress else { return }
        isThinking = true
        let snapshot = game
        Task {
            try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(350))   // feels less robotic
            let move = Minimax.bestMove(in: snapshot)
            if let move, let next = game.playing(move) { game = next }
            isThinking = false
        }
    }
}

struct GameView: View {
    @StateObject private var model = GameViewModel()
    private let columns = Array(repeating: GridItem(.flexible(), spacing: 8), count: 3)

    var body: some View {
        VStack(spacing: 20) {
            Text(status).font(.title2.bold())

            LazyVGrid(columns: columns, spacing: 8) {
                ForEach(0..<9, id: \.self) { index in
                    let mark = model.game.cells[index]
                    Button { model.tap(index) } label: {
                        Text(mark?.rawValue ?? " ")
                            .font(.system(size: 56, weight: .heavy, design: .rounded))
                            .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, minHeight: 96)
                            .background(background(for: index),
                                        in: RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12))
                    }
                    .buttonStyle(.plain)
                    .disabled(mark != nil || model.game.outcome != .inProgress)
                    .accessibilityLabel(label(for: index, mark: mark))
                }
            }
            .padding(.horizontal)

            HStack {
                Button("Undo", action: model.undo).disabled(model.game.moves.isEmpty)
                Spacer()
                Button("New Game", action: model.reset)
            }
            .padding(.horizontal)
        }
        .animation(.easeOut(duration: 0.2), value: model.game)
        .sensoryFeedback(.selection, trigger: model.game.moves.count)   // iOS 17+
    }

    private var status: String {
        switch model.game.outcome {
        case .inProgress: return model.isThinking ? "Thinking…" : "\(model.game.current.rawValue) to play"
        case .win(let p, _): return "\(p.rawValue) wins!"
        case .draw: return "It's a draw"
        }
    }

    private func background(for index: Int) -> Color {
        if case .win(_, let line) = model.game.outcome, line.contains(index) {
            return .green.opacity(0.3)
        }
        return Color(.secondarySystemBackground)
    }

    private func label(for index: Int, mark: Player?) -> String {
        let row = index / 3 + 1, column = index % 3 + 1
        return "Row \(row), column \(column), \(mark.map { $0.rawValue } ?? "empty")"
    }
}
Notice what the view  do: it never decides who won or whether a move is legal. Even the disabled state just reads the model.Version notes, since the app supports iOS 12:  is iOS 14+,  is iOS 17+, and  is iOS 16+. On an older target, use nested s, UIImpactFeedbackGenerator and , or build the same view model behind a UIKit grid. SwiftUI itself starts at iOS 13, so a genuinely iOS 12-compatible app is UIKit. The architecture doesn't change; only the view layer does.Tic tac toe is small enough to search . Minimax explores every possible continuation and assumes both players play perfectly: the computer picks the move with the best guaranteed outcome, and assumes you'll pick the one that's worst for it.enum Minimax {
    /// Best move for `game.current`, or nil if the game is over.
    static func bestMove(in game: Game) -> Int? {
        let me = game.current
        var best: (move: Int, score: Int)?
        for move in game.legalMoves {
            guard let next = game.playing(move) else { continue }
            let score = minimax(next, maximizing: false, me: me, depth: 1,
                                alpha: Int.min, beta: Int.max)
            if best == nil || score > best!.score { best = (move, score) }
        }
        return best?.move
    }

    private static func minimax(_ game: Game, maximizing: Bool, me: Player,
                                depth: Int, alpha: Int, beta: Int) -> Int {
        switch game.outcome {
        case .win(let p, _): return p == me ? 10 - depth : depth - 10   // win sooner, lose later
        case .draw: return 0
        case .inProgress: break
        }
        var alpha = alpha, beta = beta
        var best = maximizing ? Int.min : Int.max
        for move in game.legalMoves {
            guard let next = game.playing(move) else { continue }
            let score = minimax(next, maximizing: !maximizing, me: me,
                                depth: depth + 1, alpha: alpha, beta: beta)
            if maximizing {
                best = max(best, score); alpha = max(alpha, best)
            } else {
                best = min(best, score); beta = min(beta, best)
            }
            if beta <= alpha { break }      // alpha-beta pruning: this branch can't matter
        }
        return best
    }
}
Two details make this feel right: makes the AI prefer winning  over winning later, and losing later over losing now. Without it, a perfect AI sometimes "plays with its food" and looks broken. skips branches that can't change the result. The full game tree is only a few hundred thousand positions, so the AI is instant either way, but pruning is a habit worth building for bigger games.A perfect tic tac toe opponent never loses, which makes it boring quickly. A simple difficulty dial:extension Minimax {
    /// skill 0.0 = random, 1.0 = perfect
    static func move(in game: Game, skill: Double) -> Int? {
        if Double.random(in: 0..<1) > skill { return game.legalMoves.randomElement() }
        return bestMove(in: game)
    }
}
"Easy" around 0.3, "medium" around 0.7, and "hard" at 1.0 gives three genuinely different games from one function.6. Real-world example: Tic Tac Toe : Time PassAt the time of writing, its App Store listing shows:, in  (Strategy, Casual) and First released in  in the US store, averaging about Supports The listing says: "Tic Tac Toe supports one player and two player gameplay, so you can play against another human or against yourself.""Put away your pencil and paper"A value-type  model that enforces the rules (section 2)"Two player gameplay… against another human" mode: one device, alternating turns (section 4)"One player… against yourself" mode; minimax is the extension I'd add for a computer opponent (section 5)UIKit is required below iOS 13; the model is identical either way (section 4)A 3.9 average on a game this simple tells me something too. Casual players expect polish even from "time pass" games: clear win highlighting, a satisfying tap, an easy rematch. The architecture above is what makes that polish cheap to add.7. Before you ship a small game: A ChecklistRules live in one pure model, with no UI framework imports.Illegal states are impossible:  state, and validated moves that return new values.: each kind of win, a draw, illegal moves, moves after a win, and undo.The view only renders and forwards. If a view contains  logic, move it. and never on the live game. It's tested too: it should never lose from any position where a draw is possible.Difficulty is a dial, not three AIs. every cell has a spoken label with row, column and contents, and the result is announced. The fastest way to a better rating in a casual game.Tic tac toe is small, but the lessons scale. Put the rules in a value type that can't be put into an illegal state, test it like a spec, keep the view dumb, and treat the AI as just another consumer of the model, one that explores copies. The same shape works for chess, card games and anything else turn-based.Play it, or explore my other apps:What's your favourite "tiny game, big lesson" project? Tell me in the comments.

Synchronous webhook to runner systems do present some issues but we see that they are at risk for certain problems. The solution isn't to add more runners, instead what we do is we separate input functions from processing via the partitioned event bus.Partitioning effected on the basis of organization, type of repository, type and name of reference preserves arrangement of references while avoiding the creation of hot partitions for big repositories. Because there is a lack of needs of the execution process, the stateless receiver only needs to verify and publish. When queuing arises, the protocol takes a new form. As it happens, the messages can be delayed, restored, and reverted which implies that some verification must happen before the process of publishing, deduplications on the basis of delivery ID must occur at the moment of delivery and processing should also be checked once again.One must consider that there can be at least one delivery failure. In this case, the idempotent nature of keys allows for eliminating duplicates; the usage of the dead lamb queue allows for processing of bad messages without interruptions; the occurrence of the consumer lag allows for realization of the potential delivery backlogs.In a typical situation we see a report of a dependency update which in turn affects hundreds of repos in 10 minutes. The code may still do its job fine but what we see is all the repos at once putting out push and pull request events. In our case we have a sync design that is -- for auth, routing, and delivery also sync. Thus the CI receiver is hit by a lot more traffic then is usual, also with no buffer. Some requests will go over the delivery time out, some will get caught up in busy runners, and some will just disappear. Our CI receiver doesn\'t handle redelivery of broken webhooks so these lost requests may reappear in a few hours as pull requests which didn\'t get their status check.CI traffic is atypical of the fact that we are the source of our own spikes. We see mass dependency updates, a company wide rebase or a nightly job at the same cron tick for each repo which produces a trend that is perfectly in sync across a wide range of sources. AI has made this issue bigger. We are going for a 3x in developer productivity through AI support and also our agents are opening pull requests in some cases across many repos at once. Also, industry research reports that as we adopt AI more into our work flow we see better delivery throughput [2].This article discusses the workflow, which we created for the CI platform with the goal of achieving 99.99% availability. It also provides details about implementation features making it possible to achieve this level of availability and tells what mistakes we made while working on this project.Why the Synchronous Webhook Design BreaksIn the synchronous design, the source-control system sends a signed HTTP POST for each push, pull-request update, or tag [3], and the receiver validates it, selects a pipeline, and dispatches to a runner in the same request. Its response time becomes the sum of every downstream call, while GitHub expects a response within ten seconds [4]. Being able to send and receive events is not the issue that we struggle with. Our platform can process almost ten million events in a week; and traffic flow is anything but balanced: peaks are around thirty times higher than the average and happen instantaneously. Kafka can deal with such loads easily. What fails, though, is the connection between receiving and responding to an event, which results in the following four responsibilities.Burst absorption. In the lack of a buffer the receiver has to at once deal with the peak as it happens, also auto scalers do not respond fast enough to 30x peaks. Partial failures as the new normal. When working at this scale, one component is always down, which creates a bottleneck.Ordering vs throughput. Events for a particular branch or pull request have to be processed in the right order to ensure that an old event does not overwrite a new one. Global ordering is not required and cannot be achieved.Untrusted and replay-worthy input. Queued events go through delay, redelivery, or replay which present a very different set of issues as compared to a verified request at the time of issue.Adding runners does not resolve any of those problems.Figure 1 shows the pipeline: A stateless receiver which does validation and publication of events, a partitioned Kafka topic, a consumer group scheduler which uses a database of branch and pull request state, an autoscaled execution layer, a rate limited status write back path to the GitHub API, a dead letter queue and a reconciliation job.The receiver does three things: Accept requests, check their signatures, and report to Kafka. Also this does not have any other requirements in the request path which means it scales out without coordination and performs well within the platform’s timeout. We only acknowledge the webhook once Kafka has durably recorded the write; by returning success before that we actually reintroduce event loss.The partition key is the most consequential design choice. We key every event as org/repo/refType/refName, where refType is branch or pr. Events with the same key are consumed in order, while different keys are processed in parallel [5][6]. Concurrently running which is what an event's record and the builds it triggers has gone into. We found out that how the queued builds behave is a per customer configuration. In that which comes to pull requests and feature branches teams tend to batch which means a new commit may leave out older queued builds or may in fact cancel them out of the queue also it allows for the possibility of the older builds to run at the same time. On the main branch we by default run a build for every commit as teams usually want a report for each and every release or audit they do.The topic has a replication factor of 3 and holds events for a period of 60 days. Long term retention of events means that which have gone into dead letter queues may be replayed once a fix is in place, also in the time of which new consumer groups can catch up with the full history and deduplication window will equal the retention window.The scheduler is a Kafka consumer group: each instance owns a subset of partitions and the group rebalances as instances join, leave, or fail [7]. Rebalance suspends usage on the partitions that have been transferred from one place to another. Although cooperative rebalance [8] and static membership [9] minimize this process, the automatic scaling agent remains very active, as it is demonstrated in the section of the study referring to experience gained. Verifying and publishing at the edgeGitHub webhooks carry an HMAC-SHA256 digest of the raw request body in a header [10]. The receiver recomputes it over the unparsed bytes and compares in constant time [11], because a non-constant-time comparison leaks timing information an attacker can use to forge a signature.async function verifyAndPublish(rawBody, headers, secret) {
 // Header format is "sha256=<hex digest of raw body>"
 const expected = 'sha256=' + hmacSha256Hex(secret, rawBody);
 if (!constantTimeEquals(expected, headers['x-hub-signature-256'])) {
   return reject(401);
 }
 const event = normalize(parse(rawBody));
 event.deliveryId = headers['x-github-delivery'];   // dedup key for replays
 // refType is "branch" or "pr"; ordering is preserved per key
 const key = [event.org, event.repo, event.refType, event.refName].join('/');
 await publish('ci.build.events', key, event);      // wait for durable ack
 return accept(202);                                // queued, not processed
}
The await on publish matters most: the producer waits for acknowledgment from the in-sync replicas and if Kafka is unavailable the receiver returns an error rather than accept an event it cannot store.The offset is what Kafka uses for “to date all has been processed. Post processing commit provides at least once delivery [12]. Exactly once semantics break at the first external call [13] which is when the scheduler puts together a deterministic idempotent key for each processing job out of repo, commit SHA, pipeline, and trigger, also the execution layer which we put in to play for duplicate prevention.Commit time goes up as we see unexpected order of events due to debounce (which is explained later). By default at the end of the process the Spring Kafka does a commit. But in our case the debounce listener goes off before the processing does which means that in the off chance of a crash during that delay the event may be lost. This can be fixed if we do the acknowledgment manually which in turn allows the code to do the commit only after we are really done with the processing.Manual backs alone still allow out-of-order commits. If offset 100 is still waiting while 101 and 102 finish, committing 102 and then crashing loses event 100. Spring Kafka's asyncAcks prevents this by deferring each commit until every earlier offset has been acknowledged [14]. A simplified version:factory.getContainerProperties().setAckMode(AckMode.MANUAL);
factory.getContainerProperties().setAsyncAcks(true);
@KafkaListener(topics = "ci.build.events")
void onEvent(BuildEvent event, Acknowledgment ack) {
   if (event.isBranch() && !event.isDefaultBranch()) {
       // Debounce off the listener thread; the partition keeps flowing
       delayed.schedule(() -> {
           resolve(event);
           ack.acknowledge();
       }, 2, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
   } else {
       handle(event);
       ack.acknowledge();
   }
}
Deduplicating branch and pull-request buildsA push to the head branch of a pull request generates an event for the branch and a separate event for the pull request, meaning that they can get delivered in any order. It would be wasteful to build two separate events for each pull request. Upon getting a branch event for any branch that is not the default one the scheduler puts it on hold for a 2 second period and then checks its db for any present pull request which has the same branch name at its head. When it identifies a proper pull request it goes forward without the need to build out the branch, instead just the head commit SHA is updated, which is handled by the pull-request pipeline for the actual build. Also if the pull request’s status is not known at that point the scheduler does a slower but more accurate query to the GitHub API to determine if there is in fact an open pull request. This also helps to avoid duplicate builds, a thing that does not play out at all when using the default branch which in turn can be put forth as the most active branch in the monorepo.In the resolution of out-of-order events, timestamps prove to be very useful at keeping events in chronological order and at the same time assuring present moment information is reliable. This system helps assign timestamps to each event transformation, depending on the timing of the event itself determined by the type of event, such as the branch event timestamp or timestamp of the last update in case of a PR event. In instances when an event falls under the out-of-order event category due to being older than the event state preserved so far, it is rejected. Delivery time cannot be utilized in this scenario, as in such cases the event while being delayed or undelivered may seem to occur after, while commit dates cannot be used either, as newly created commit timestamps may contradict the actual timestamp of zero commit.Sizing the Partition CountIn the issue of out-of-order events timestamps play a key role in maintaining chronological accuracy of events at the same time they see to it that we receive reliable info in the present moment. This system helps assign timestamps to each event transformation, depending on the timing of the event itself determined by the type of event, such as the branch event timestamp or timestamp of the last update in case of a PR event. In instances when an event falls under the out-of-order event category due to being older than the event state preserved so far, it is rejected. Delivery time cannot be utilized in this scenario, as in such cases the event while being delayed or undelivered may seem to occur after, while commit dates cannot be used either, as newly created commit timestamps may contradict the actual timestamp of zero commit.Baseline: divide observed peak load by 20 events/second to get the minimum partition count.Skew allowance (1.5x) for busy organizations and refs.Growth headroom (3x), matching the AI productivity target. Partitions cannot be removed, and adding them later changes the key-to-partition mapping.Round up to a power of two. We use 128 partitions, which gives roughly 2,500 events per second of scheduling capacity and allows up to 128 scheduler instances, though far fewer normally run.Partition out count, consumer parallelism, execution layer autoscale and tenant isolation. These have to be tuned in tandem. Four elements which determine if this architecture scales are partition count, consumer parallelism, execution layer autoscaling and tenant isolation.Partition count caps consumer parallelism, because a consumer group can have no more active members than the topic has partitions [6]. Choose it with headroom. By adding partitions later on, keys will be remapped and the ordering per-reference will be briefly disrupted, plus the number of partitions cannot be minimized. Consumer batching which processes multiple events per poll instead of one at a time increases the throughput per consumer but at the same time causes higher latency per event in lightly loaded conditions. This should be made a variable instead of a hard code. Autoscaling should follow leading signals. Consumers should be scaled based on the lag of the consumers and the runners based on the depth of the pending builds queue, instead of measured CPU or memory usage. At that point, the resource metrics will show saturation, with an upstream request backlog already in place.In terms of the isolation of tenants, it is important to note that hash partitioning involves a lot of tenants in each partition meaning that the burst of one of the enterprises might delay all the rest.In durable queues, whatever reaches the bus remains for a time of 60 days and can be retried or replayed:Only confirmed events can enter the bus. The receiver checks the signature of each one before sending it to publish [10][11] and it is the only entity that can write to the build-event topic. A consumer should not be the first checker of an event.However signatures do not protect against replay. The HMAC does not have a timestamp, which means that a request can be obtained in determination for an unlimited time. We deduplicate according to the delivery ID for the entire retention window.Authorize again at the moment of execution. Minutes may pass between acceptance and execution which is why the scheduler checks that the actor has access, that the ref is to the commit, and that the fork policy allows the build.Take care of not disclosing secrets in events. Credentials are received from the secrets manager at the moment of execution because events can be replayed or exposed.The first sign which appears is consumer lag. Kafka, in this case, directly reflects it [15] and before the errors and latency. The correlation ID, which is passed from the webhook to the status write-back [16], presents the answer to the question "why didn't this build get a status?"The dead letter queue must come before that one and the problem with the first poison-pill event arises. A message which has a status issue can block everything that is in the partition line for the queue. After a number of retries it is moved to the dead letter queue meaning that there must be its own monitoring and a checking process in the dead letter queue. It means that if the retention period is over sixty days then it is possible for us to repeat the events once we have the fix.Back pressure. When the execution layer is overloaded or the debounce pool is full then consumers are forced to yellow their partitions and let Kafka take care of the backlog and avoid memory use from consumers.Limits on service requests. GitHub has set limits on the number of service requests made by a particular organization [17], but both the callback and pull requests can be counted as service requests dependent on those limits. In the case of our particular GitHub application, it can be installed to each organization separately so the requests made by one organization can never affect the limit of another organization. Data validation. There are some events that are never delivered by GitHub [1], which means the system never receives them. In this way, there is a special job that compares current pushes with the requests already recorded in the system and generates the missing requests.Trade-offs and LimitationsAfter a webhook comes in the status check may take a bit to show. Also the event bus becomes a primary dependency. If it goes out the whole pipeline grinds to a halt, not just a single request.In an organization that runs through a few hundred builds a day this architecture is overkill. At that scale the simple, easy to debug and economical nature of a synchronous design is what is required and a partitioned bus with a consumer group scheduler just adds to the cost and issues without bettering the product. The crossover point is not a set number. It is when you see burst absorption issues, partial failures which aren’t isolated, or order at scale becoming a regular ops issue instead of a what if scenario.Production Lessons LearnedRebalance storms are not a theory—they are a real problem. Every time a scaling event happens the consumer group rebalances and consumption stops on the affected partitions until the reassignment finishes [7]. If a scheduler scales frequently it spends most of its time rebalancing instead of actually processing messages. From the outside it looks like the system is stuck even though every consumer is running fine. Tuning the rebalance protocol didn’t help much. What really made a difference was changing how we autoscale—scale up, scale down slowly and make sure there’s a cooldown period between each scaling event.Migrate customers out in batches, at once we did not do a full scale move of all at once. The old system did not have an event bus; it was the sync design which went before and was the only go to live option for all teams, which also did not allow for down time. Instead of a full scale switch over we put customers into the new pipeline in batches:.Dependency analysis was done to see what the old service did which documentation didn't report. We started with a small set of what we thought to be low risk organizations for the batch migration and only grew each batch once the previous one went through without issue.Also we had a pre determined roll back plan for any batch which may have to be moved back to the old system without having to improvise.Batching kept the blast radius small. A problem in the new pipeline affected only the organizations already moved and issues from monorepos and high-volume organizations surfaced before they could reach everyone.Rehearse failures before production. Hot partitions, rebalance storms and poison-pill events are far cheaper to find on purpose than in an incident. Two forms of validation are worth building in. First we will run through a set of anonymized production webhooks which includes bursts on the staging environment because synthetic uniform load does not present the issue at hand which it should. Second we will introduce the failures which the design is intended to weather out[18]:Kill a consumer in the middle of a batch to see that no event is lost and that redelivered duplicates are rejected by idempotency keys. Put forth a malformed event to have it land in the dead letter queue instead of causing its partition to stall. Scale the consumer group up and down to see what amount of lag a rebalance at the current partition count adds.The design of the synchronous webhook is appropriate for the workload that most companies run. However, the trouble starts when groups believe they can scale the webhook by increasing the number of runners. Long before the execution capability becomes a limiting factor, changes need to be made at the ingestion level: before the execution capacity is reached the structure should change: there should be *stateless receivers for verification and publication*, partitioned announcement tool, horizontally scalable groups of consumers for planning the workload, and the collection of undelivered events to control the processing of batched tasks. All of these components are well-known. The trick lies in bringing them together in a way appropriate for the requirements generated by the work of the software (including the requirements for putting the records in their proper order, authorizing access to the information, and isolating failures).That which we see is coming faster for more teams. We see in industry research a greater distance growing between what organizations do with CI/CD as a core infrastructure as opposed to a utility issue [2] also in play is AI in development which is increasing build volume still. We will put the bus forward as a source of operational signal as we do for a scheduling issue. We will add a structured failure classification field at publish time which will turn each build failure event into a data point for the auto diagnostician, we do away with a separate log scraping pipeline. As more organizations make this transition we expect to see shared and proven tooling for CI ingestion to come out rather than each team having to piece together from general purpose messaging we did.

For this project I wanted something new. Something that involves art. A new type of art. I wanted to combine mathematics visuals with formulas so you can experiment and create your own art. You change the numbers and exponents, add z or c, and create your own art. Your imagination is the limit. What I created is a fractal laboratory. Download it as a PNG or SVG. In simple terms, fractal art is digital art created using mathematical formulas and repeated patterns rather than drawing every shape manually.In another way, fractal art is a form of art that uses mathematics to display perfect geometric shapes. That could not be created manually. The fun part of the code:<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Responsive Mathematical Fractal Generator</title>
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjs/11.8.0/math.js"></script>
  <style>
    :root {
      --bg-color: #0f111a;
      --panel-bg: #1e2230;
      --accent-color: #6366f1;
      --accent-hover: #4f46e5;
      --text-color: #f3f4f6;
      --text-muted: #9ca3af;
      --border-color: #374151;
    }

    * {
      box-sizing: border-box;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    }

    body {
      background-color: var(--bg-color);
      color: var(--text-color);
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      min-height: 100vh;
      min-height: 100dvh; /* Dynamic viewport height for mobile browsers */
      overflow: x-hidden;
    }

    /* Responsive Main Layout (Column on Mobile, Row on Desktop) */
    .app-container {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      flex: 1;
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
    }

    @media (min-width: 850px) {
      .app-container {
        flex-direction: row;
        height: 100vh;
        overflow: hidden;
      }
    }

    /* Sidebar controls */
    .sidebar {
      width: 100%;
      background-color: var(--panel-bg);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
      padding: 16px;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 14px;
    }

    @media (min-width: 850px) {
      .sidebar {
        width: 340px;
        min-width: 340px;
        border-bottom: none;
        border-right: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        padding: 20px;
        overflow-y: auto;
      }
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: #fff;
    }

    .input-group {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    label {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--text-muted);
      font-weight: 500;
    }

    input[type="text"], input[type="number"], select {
      background: #11131f;
      border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
      color: var(--text-color);
      padding: 10px;
      border-radius: 6px;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      outline: none;
      width: 100%;
    }

    input[type="text"]:focus, input[type="number"]:focus, select:focus {
      border-color: var(--accent-color);
    }

    .btn {
      background-color: var(--accent-color);
      color: white;
      border: none;
      padding: 12px;
      border-radius: 6px;
      font-weight: 600;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: background 0.2s;
      width: 100%;
      touch-action: manipulation;
    }

    .btn:hover {
      background-color: var(--accent-hover);
    }

    .btn-secondary {
      background-color: transparent;
      border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
      color: var(--text-color);
    }

    .btn-secondary:hover {
      background-color: #2a2f45;
    }

    .button-row {
      display: flex;
      gap: 8px;
    }

    .download-section {
      display: flex;
      gap: 10px;
      margin-top: 6px;
    }

    @media (min-width: 850px) {
      .download-section {
        margin-top: auto;
      }
    }

    /* Canvas Viewport */
    .main-viewport {
      flex: 1;
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: center;
      position: relative;
      background: #090a0f;
      padding: 12px;
      min-height: 350px;
    }

    .canvas-container {
      position: relative;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 600px;
      aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
      display: flex;
      justify-content: center;
      align-items: center;
    }

    canvas {
      width: 100% !important;
      height: 100% !important;
      box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
      border-radius: 6px;
      touch-action: none;
    }

    .status-overlay {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px;
      right: 10px;
      background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
      padding: 6px 12px;
      border-radius: 16px;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      color: var(--text-muted);
      pointer-events: none;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <div class="app-container">
    <div class="sidebar">
      <h1>Fractal Generator</h1>

      <div class="input-group">
        <label for="formula">Formula f(z, c):</label>
        <input type="text" id="formula" value="z^2 + c" placeholder="e.g. z^2 + c">
      </div>

      <div class="input-group">
        <label for="type">Fractal Type:</label>
        <select id="type">
          <option value="mandelbrot">Mandelbrot Style (Vary c)</option>
          <option value="julia">Julia Set (Fixed c, Vary z)</option>
        </select>
      </div>

      <div class="input-group" id="julia-params" style="display: none;">
        <label>Constant c (Real, Imaginary):</label>
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 8px;">
          <input type="number" id="c_real" value="-0.7" step="0.05" placeholder="Real">
          <input type="number" id="c_imag" value="0.27015" step="0.05" placeholder="Imag">
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="input-group">
        <label for="maxIter">Resolution / Iterations:</label>
        <input type="number" id="maxIter" value="50" min="10" max="250">
      </div>

      <div class="input-group">
        <label for="colorPalette">Color Palette:</label>
        <select id="colorPalette">
          <option value="electric">Electric Neon</option>
          <option value="fire">Fire & Gold</option>
          <option value="ocean">Deep Ocean</option>
          <option value="grayscale">Monochrome</option>
        </select>
      </div>

      <div class="button-row">
        <button class="btn" id="renderBtn">Render</button>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="resetBtn">Reset</button>
      </div>

      <div class="download-section">
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="downloadPNG">PNG</button>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary" id="downloadSVG">SVG</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="main-viewport">
      <div class="canvas-container">
        <div class="status-overlay" id="status">Ready</div>
        <canvas id="fractalCanvas" width="500" height="500"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    const canvas = document.getElementById('fractalCanvas');
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
    const statusEl = document.getElementById('status');

    let zoom = 1;
    let centerX = 0;
    let centerY = 0;

    // Toggle Julia controls
    document.getElementById('type').addEventListener('change', (e) => {
      document.getElementById('julia-params').style.display = 
        e.target.value === 'julia' ? 'flex' : 'none';
    });

    // Color Palettes
    function getColor(iter, maxIter, palette) {
      if (iter === maxIter) return [0, 0, 0];

      let t = iter / maxIter;

      if (palette === 'electric') {
        return [
          Math.sin(t * 10) * 127 + 128,
          Math.cos(t * 15) * 127 + 128,
          Math.sin(t * 25) * 127 + 128
        ];
      } else if (palette === 'fire') {
        return [
          Math.min(255, iter * 8),
          Math.min(255, iter * 3),
          Math.min(255, iter * 0.5)
        ];
      } else if (palette === 'ocean') {
        return [
          Math.sin(t * 5) * 50,
          Math.sin(t * 10) * 100 + 100,
          Math.sin(t * 20) * 127 + 128
        ];
      } else {
        let val = Math.floor((iter / maxIter) * 255);
        return [val, val, val];
      }
    }

    // Main Render Loop
    function renderFractal() {
      statusEl.innerText = "Rendering...";

      setTimeout(() => {
        const formulaStr = document.getElementById('formula').value;
        const fractalType = document.getElementById('type').value;
        const maxIter = parseInt(document.getElementById('maxIter').value);
        const palette = document.getElementById('colorPalette').value;

        const cRealFixed = parseFloat(document.getElementById('c_real').value);
        const cImagFixed = parseFloat(document.getElementById('c_imag').value);

        let compiledExpr;
        try {
          compiledExpr = math.compile(formulaStr);
        } catch (err) {
          alert("Invalid Formula: " + err.message);
          statusEl.innerText = "Error";
          return;
        }

        const width = canvas.width;
        const height = canvas.height;
        const imgData = ctx.createImageData(width, height);
        const data = imgData.data;

        const scale = 3.0 / (zoom * Math.min(width, height));

        for (let py = 0; py < height; py++) {
          for (let px = 0; px < width; px++) {

            let x0 = (px - width / 2) * scale + centerX;
            let y0 = (py - height / 2) * scale + centerY;

            let z, c;
            if (fractalType === 'mandelbrot') {
              z = math.complex(0, 0);
              c = math.complex(x0, y0);
            } else {
              z = math.complex(x0, y0);
              c = math.complex(cRealFixed, cImagFixed);
            }

            let iter = 0;
            let escaped = false;

            while (iter < maxIter) {
              try {
                z = compiledExpr.evaluate({ z: z, c: c });
                if ((z.re * z.re + z.im * z.im) > 4.0) {
                  escaped = true;
                  break;
                }
              } catch (e) {
                escaped = true;
                break;
              }
              iter++;
            }

            const [r, g, b] = getColor(iter, maxIter, palette);
            const pixelIdx = (py * width + px) * 4;
            data[pixelIdx] = r;
            data[pixelIdx + 1] = g;
            data[pixelIdx + 2] = b;
            data[pixelIdx + 3] = 255;
          }
        }

        ctx.putImageData(imgData, 0, 0);
        statusEl.innerText = "Done";
      }, 20);
    }

    // Export Handlers
    document.getElementById('downloadPNG').addEventListener('click', () => {
      const link = document.createElement('a');
      link.download = 'fractal.png';
      link.href = canvas.toDataURL('image/png');
      link.click();
    });

    document.getElementById('downloadSVG').addEventListener('click', () => {
      const dataURL = canvas.toDataURL('image/png');
      const svgString = 
`<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="${canvas.width}" height="${canvas.height}">
  <image href="${dataURL}" width="${canvas.width}" height="${canvas.height}" />
</svg>`;

      const blob = new Blob([svgString], { type: 'image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8' });
      const link = document.createElement('a');
      link.download = 'fractal.svg';
      link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      link.click();
    });

    // Control Listeners
    document.getElementById('renderBtn').addEventListener('click', renderFractal);

    document.getElementById('resetBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
      zoom = 1;
      centerX = 0;
      centerY = 0;
      renderFractal();
    });

    // Zoom on Click / Tap
    canvas.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
      const clickX = (e.clientX - rect.left) * (canvas.width / rect.width);
      const clickY = (e.clientY - rect.top) * (canvas.height / rect.height);

      const scale = 3.0 / (zoom * Math.min(canvas.width, canvas.height));
      centerX += (clickX - canvas.width / 2) * scale;
      centerY += (clickY - canvas.height / 2) * scale;
      zoom *= 2;

      renderFractal();
    });

    // Initial render
    renderFractal();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
Explaining parts of the code: <input type="text" id="formula" value="z^2 + c">
<select id="type">

  <option value="mandelbrot">Mandelbrot Style (Vary c)</option>

  <option value="julia">Julia Set (Fixed c, Vary z)</option>

</select>
Lets the user switch between two mathematical approaches. <input type="number" id="maxIter" value="50" min="10" max="250">
Controls how many mathematical iterations are performed for each pixel.<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjs/11.8.0/math.js"></script>
It loads MathJS, which allows the webpage to understand mathematical expressions. The renderFractal() functionThis is the core of the project.function renderFractal() {
The pixel-by-pixel calculation: for (let py = 0; py < height; py++) {

  for (let px = 0; px < width; px++) {
And if you want to try it yourself here is a demo. Artists can experiment with new formulas to create their own. I even experimented with a couple of formulas. Research / experimentationIt could serve as a simple visualization environment for experimenting with families of interactive equations. UI developers can use the results for webpages to display them on.In general, everyone is interested in art. Or this type of art. Start by simple formulas for example: And expand with each step to become something like The purpose of the project is to show that mathematics could be used for very beautiful types of art. It's easy and fun and could show the beauty of mathematics. This project shows that art has many ways to be implemented. It's fun and could be done by anyone. Artist, programmers, and anyone interested. And now you can also create your own type of art. With this project.

In September 2024, War Robots support received a surge of complaints from Chinese iOS players: after updating to iOS 18, in-game text was displaying incorrectly for some users. The issue manifested differently across devices: some players noticed missing characters, while others reported wrong or unfamiliar glyph styles. To make matters worse, we were unable to reproduce the bug on our local test devices.My name is Sergey Madekin, Senior Developer on War Robots. In this article, I will share how we ultimately resolved the missing Chinese characters issue.Investigating the Root CauseUpon investigation, we discovered that our Chinese text rendering relied directly on system fonts. The game uses standard Unity UI (Text) components configured with dynamic fonts. Unity first searches for required glyphs within the designated font asset, then moves through the Font Names list, and finally falls back to the engine's built-in list of fallback system fonts for the given platform. It was this fallback mechanism that produced inconsistent results across different devices running iOS 18.A similar issue with missing characters in Legacy Text on iOS 18+ is logged in the Unity Issue Tracker. At the time of writing, no fix version is specified in the ticket.The First Attempt: Adding the Full FontThe fix itself is straightforward: bundle a dedicated Chinese font into the build and configure it as a fallback for missing glyphs. However, a complete Chinese font file contains tens of thousands of characters. Our base Noto Sans SC TTF file weighed around 10 MB, and adding it increased the final build size by 37 MB.We initially verified the fix by releasing a build with the full font, keeping in mind that we would optimize it later if it proved effective. The update resolved all text rendering issues with no further complaints, so I moved on to reducing the file size. Fortunately, daily communication requires only a fraction of the full character set, and a specific game requires even fewer.Optimization and Font Subsetting with pyftsubsetThe next challenge was keeping only the necessary characters in the source file. Luckily, the right tool already exists: pyftsubset, a command-line utility from Python's fontTools package designed to create slimmed-down font files containing only specified glyph sets. It discards unused glyphs and metadata, drastically shrinking the file size.All that remained was identifying which characters to keep. I started with a standard frequency list of common Chinese characters and ran the subsetting tool against it. However, gaps immediately appeared — particularly in localized in-game names, and there were quite a few of them. It became clear that we needed to extract the exact character set used in our text resources. I pulled all current translations and compiled a list of every unique character found in them. I then ran pyftsubset on the source file, keeping only this set. The results were impressive: out of roughly 30,000 characters in the original font, only about 3,000 remained, dropping the TTF size from 10 MB down to around 900 KB. The resulting build size increase was just 1.24 MB.Edge Cases and Player NicknamesWe shipped the next release with this lightweight font. Player complaints dropped drastically, but a few remained. We discovered that certain configuration files contained unlocalized strings with rare characters. Additionally, some players reported missing characters in their custom usernames.Because the missing character count was low, I created a dedicated supplementary list. While this resolved all known edge cases, it doesn't guarantee full coverage for future usernames containing obscure characters. Moving forward, whenever a new missing character is reported, we simply append it to the supplementary list and regenerate the font.Automating the Font Pipeline in UnitySince game content updates frequently, we needed an easy, automated way to update our character set before each release.After a few iterations, we built a custom Unity Editor tool that automates the whole process in a single click:Connects to the target test server hosting localization files for the upcoming release.Downloads the latest text resources.Extracts unique characters from the text and merges them with our supplementary list.Excludes glyphs already present in the primary game font to avoid duplication.Verifies character availability in the source Chinese TTF file. Missing glyphs are logged and skipped.(Optional) Includes the character set from the previous release if the "preserve old characters" setting is enabled.Runs pyftsubset on the source TTF file using the compiled character list and outputs the optimized font directly into the build.Preserving the previous release's character set prevents regression bugs if strings are removed from current localization files. While this causes the font size to grow slightly over time, it provides a solid balance between file footprint and backward compatibility.The Unicode Pitfall: One Character, Two CharsWhile refining the generator, we uncovered another nuance: collecting target characters using a  is unsafe.In C#, strings are UTF-16 encoded. Most Chinese characters we use fit into a single , but certain rare ones require two. Together, these two units form a surrogate pair representing a single character.A  stores individual unit values without preserving pairs. For instance, two distinct rare characters might share the same high surrogate. During deduplication, that shared unit is kept only once—reducing four  values down to a set of three. When passing that corrupted set to  as a string, neither of the original characters will be fully preserved.This behavior is demonstrated below:string text = "\U00020000\U00020001"; // Two distinct characters: 𠀀 and 𠀁

Console.WriteLine(text.Length);                   // 4 — char count
Console.WriteLine(new HashSet<char>(text).Count); // 3 — unique char units (corrupted)
Console.WriteLine(GetTextScalars(text).Count);   // 2 — actual Unicode characters
To preserve surrogate pairs intact, we introduced the  method. It iterates through the string and populates a  with complete characters: standard ones as a single- string, and surrogate pairs as a two- string.private static HashSet<string> GetTextScalars(string text)
{
    var result = new HashSet<string>();
    for (var i = 0; i < text.Length; i++)
    {
        if (char.IsHighSurrogate(text[i]) && i + 1 < text.Length && char.IsLowSurrogate(text[i + 1]))
        {
            result.Add(text.Substring(i, 2));
            i++;
        }
        else
        {
            result.Add(text[i].ToString());
        }
    }

    return result;
}
This safeguard protects our character set during subset preparation. Note that it is unrelated to the original iOS system font fallback issue.By building this workflow, we gained the ability to quickly update and expand our Chinese font while keeping the build size impact minimal. It now covers all game localizations, handles custom player names via our supplementary list, and optionally preserves coverage across past releases.

Most articles about database migrations assume you know the final model before you start. In real products, you often don't. You ship a design, it goes to production, and then a customer conversation tells you the design was wrong.In 2026, I designed and built a speaker roles feature for an event platform, end to end: database schema, GraphQL API, and changes to both the organizer dashboard and the attendee-facing event app. I shipped it as a one-to-one relationship. After it was live, the requirement changed to many-to-many. This is how I evolved the data model twice without breaking a single existing event, and what I'd ask earlier next time.The problem: a custom field doing a feature's jobOrganizers wanted to label what each speaker does in a session: moderator, host, interviewer. The platform had no concept for this, so organizers worked around it with a generic custom field on the speaker profile. It was free text, it was manual, and nothing in the product understood it.The goal was to replace that workaround with a real feature: organizers define roles per event and assign them to speakers.The constraint was the hard part. Existing events were already live, with speakers, integrations, and API clients built on the old data model. None of them could break while the new feature rolled out incrementally.Version 1: one role per speaker, zero breaking changesThe first design was simple. I added a  table, scoped to an event, and linked each speaker to one role through a foreign key.A foreign key creates an obvious problem: every existing speaker now needs a valid role, and so does every speaker created by a client that has never heard of roles. Three decisions solved it:Every event gets a default role, automatically. When an event is created, the backend creates its default role in the same flow. No event can exist without one.Existing speakers were migrated to their event's default role. A migration script assigned them before anything depended on the new relationship. is optional in the API. If a client doesn't send it, the backend assigns the event's default role.The third one is what kept existing integrations working. In the GraphQL schema, it looks like this:type SpeakerRole {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  isDefault: Boolean!
}

input CreateSpeakerInput {
  eventId: ID!
  name: String!
  # Optional on purpose: older clients don't know roles exist
  roleId: ID
}
const roleId =
  input.roleId ?? (await speakerRoles.getDefaultRoleId(input.eventId));
One line, but it carries the whole compatibility strategy. Clients that know about roles send one. Clients that don't keep working, and their speakers still end up in a valid state. Nobody had to coordinate a release with us.The data question: migrate the old field or not?During implementation, a product question came up. Organizers already had speaker types in that custom field. Should the event app keep showing it, or should the new roles replace it? Showing both would display the same information twice.The tempting answer was to migrate the custom field into the new roles. Before proposing that, I checked production data. Only a small minority of speakers had the custom field filled in. And because it was free text, the values varied too much to map reliably to roles. An automatic migration would have produced wrong roles for real events.So instead of migrating, I proposed a toggle. Each organizer chooses whether the event app displays the old custom field or the new speaker roles. Organizers who depended on the old field lost nothing. Everyone else got the new feature.The lesson: a migration is only as good as the data you migrate. When the source data is unreliable, giving users a choice can be safer than guessing for them.Version 2: the requirement changed after launchThe backend was fully in production when a meeting with the integrations team surfaced something new: a customer needed speakers to hold multiple roles in the same session. A speaker could be both host and interviewer in the same session.The one-to-one design couldn't express that. A speaker pointed to exactly one role.The quick fix would have been to duplicate the speaker, one row per role. That didn't work either. The speaker table had a unique index, and other features relied on a speaker existing once. Breaking that assumption would have spread the change far beyond roles.The honest conclusion was that the model needed a redesign: a pivot table between speakers and roles, a many-to-many relationship. The real question was how to get there while Version 1 was live and in use.Migrating to many-to-many without changing the APII didn't replace the old relationship. I added the new one next to it and moved traffic over in steps: links a speaker to a role. The old foreign key stays in place.Start writing to the new model. The organizer dashboard populates the pivot table on every role change. A migration script copies every existing speaker-role link into the pivot table.Read new first, fall back to old. A dataloader resolves roles from the pivot table and falls back to the old relationship when no assignment exists yet. It reads the new relationship, and the API response shape stays the same.The old foreign key stays in Version 2, so reads can fall back to it for any speaker not yet in the pivot table.Because of that, the backfill didn't need to finish before deploying, and no event ever had a window where its speakers lost their roles.What I ask before designing a relationship nowLooking back, Version 1 wasn't a mistake. It solved the problem as it was described at the time. But a few questions, asked earlier, would have saved a redesign:"Can this ever be more than one?" Ask it about every one-to-one relationship, and ask the people closest to customers, not only product. The integrations team knew before anyone else.What happens to clients that don't know this feature exists? Make new inputs optional and give them a safe default.Can every existing record satisfy the new constraint? If not, create the defaults and migrate before you enforce it.Is the data I'm about to migrate trustworthy? Check production before promising a migration.Can old and new models coexist? If reads fall back gracefully, the backfill stops being a deadline.The hardest part of this feature wasn't the schema or the GraphQL types. It was that production never pauses while you change your mind. Existing events, old integrations, and real organizers were using Version 1 the whole time I built Version 2.What made it work was treating every change as a transition instead of a replacement: defaults for clients that don't know about roles, a toggle instead of an unreliable migration, and a dataloader that could read both models at once.The question I ask now isn't only "Does the new feature work?" It's "Can the system move safely from where it is to where it needs to be, even if where it needs to be changes halfway through?"

 BuildProof is an open-source registry on Base mainnet where several parties co-sign the SHA-256 of a file. One of the file types is an AI-output manifest: the hashes of the prompt, the input documents and the model's answer, bundled into one canonical JSON. The AI operator signs it, a human reviewer signs or rejects it, and anyone can later check a file against the chain for free, or let an agent pay $0.01 in USDC per lookup over x402. Code, contract and endpoints are live. It has no real users yet; everything on mainnet is demo data.The problem: AI output has no paper trailI direct a construction and real-estate development company. Every month the same paperwork moves between the client, the general contractor, subcontractors and technical supervision: acceptance acts, invoices, supplementary agreements that change a quantity or a unit rate. It travels as PDFs over e-mail and messengers. Two arguments come up again and again: which version was signed, and does this act actually match the contract and its amendments.Now add AI to that flow. A model summarizes an NDA, flags risks in a contract, checks the arithmetic in an act. A lawyer or an engineer reads the result and approves it. A few weeks later someone disputes it, and the questions are the old ones with a new twist:"That's not what the model said." The output file was edited after review, by the operator, by the reviewer, or by whoever forwarded it. Nothing inside a .md file records what it said yesterday."That's not what I approved." The reviewer says they saw a different version. Without a shared record, it is one mailbox against another."The model saw a different contract." The answer may be correct for an input that was swapped. If the inputs are not pinned, the output is not either. An agent that is about to pay an invoice or accept a contract version on the strength of another agent's report has no way to ask whether a human ever signed off on that exact report.None of this needs a sophisticated attacker. One changed number and two people with different files are enough.The approach: pin the bytes, not the contentThe idea is deliberately boring. Hash the files locally. Put only the hash on-chain, together with the list of wallets that must sign. The record becomes  only when every listed wallet has signed. A required signer can instead , and the rejection carries the SHA-256 of the reason (for example a review report), so the reason text stays off-chain but is pinned too. When an amendment to an attested document is attested, the original is marked .For AI output, the "file" is a manifest. The CLI builds it from four things:node cli/buildproof.mjs manifest --model gpt-4o \
  --prompt examples/legal/ai_review_prompt.txt \
  --input examples/legal/nda_mutual.pdf \
  --output examples/legal/ai_review_output.md \
  --created 2026-10-09 > examples/legal/ai_review_manifest.json
and the result in the repository is a single canonical JSON line (keys in fixed alphabetical order, no trailing newline, so the file hashes the same on every machine):{"created":"2026-10-09","input_documents_sha256":["0xd4caea07…8b3a"],"model":"gpt-4o",
 "output_sha256":"0x92edd666…2d36","prompt_sha256":"0x0216b2a6…1c3f",
 "reviewed_by":"human reviewer (wallet signs below)","schema":"buildproof.ai-output/v1"}
(Hashes shortened here; the full file is examples/legal/ai_review_manifest.json.) The manifest is then registered with two signers, the AI operator and the human reviewer. This exact example is live on Base mainnet: attestation , registered in 0x38c7eef6…, signed by the AI operator's key offline (EIP-712) and relayed in 0x8662ee13…, then signed by the human reviewer in 0xf779a3e5…. Status: . Change one byte of the prompt, of any input, or of the answer, and the manifest hash changes, so the lookup returns .Be precise about what this proves. It proves which exact bytes each wallet approved, and when. It does  prove that the named model produced the output:  is a string the operator writes. Proving model execution is a different problem (TEEs, zkML) and out of scope here. (Solidity, 228 lines) on Base mainnet at 0xF818e4A95BBA02c822bCa8A0CFB50a2Ae4B8eE94. No owner, no constructor arguments, no upgrade path. Source verified on Sourcify (exact match). A record holds the hash, a document type, a project reference, an optional link to the document it amends, and up to 16 required signers. (Node): , , , , , , , , . on GitHub Pages. It hashes the file in the browser with WebCrypto and reads the chain through a public RPC. The file never leaves the machine. (Cloudflare Worker): GET /v1/attestation/{sha256} returns a verdict (, , , , , ), the signers and the dates, for $0.01 USDC over x402. The same lookup code answers on the NEAR AI Agent Market and in the OpenServ x402 catalogue.An honest footnote on decentralization: the registry is permissionless and readable for free through any RPC; the Worker is a convenience I run on Cloudflare. If I shut it down tomorrow, every record stays readable on Base.The contract, in two fragmentsSigners who have no gas can still sign. They produce an EIP-712 signature over the attestation id and the document hash, and anyone relays it:function signBySig(bytes32 id, address signer, bytes calldata signature) external {
    Attestation storage a = _att[id];
    if (a.status == Status.None) revert UnknownAttestation(id);
    bytes32 digest = signDigest(id);
    if (_recover(digest, signature) != signer) revert BadSignature();
    _sign(id, signer, true);
}
The domain separator includes  and the contract address,  rejects high- (malleable) signatures and , and the id embeds the document hash, so a signature cannot be replayed onto another document, contract or chain.The second fragment came from a review. Two amendments to the same attested contract could both be pending; whichever completed last would silently overwrite the first. The fix is one line in :if (a.signedCount == a.signerCount) {
    a.status = Status.Attested;
    a.closedAt = uint64(block.timestamp);
    emit Attested(id, a.docHash);
    bytes32 old = a.supersedes;
    if (old != bytes32(0)) {
        if (supersededBy[old] != bytes32(0)) revert BadSupersedes(old);
        supersededBy[old] = id;
        emit Superseded(old, id);
    }
}
Anyone can register any hash with any signer list, so a stranger can front-run a registration with fake signers. That does not block the real record: the id is keccak256(docHash, docType, projectRef, supersedes, signers, msg.sender), so the fake one gets a different id. Verifiers pass the expected parties (), and the verdict only counts records whose signer set matches exactly.The Foundry suite has 21 tests, two of them fuzz tests, covering replay, malleability, griefing registrations, double signing and the supersession race.The interesting user of a verify endpoint is not a person with a browser. It is an agent in the middle of a task: "before I pay this invoice, was this act co-signed by both parties?" or "before I forward this AI review to the client, did a human sign it?" Agents do not fill in sign-up forms, so the API has no accounts and no keys. Payment is the authentication. The route config is small:paymentMiddleware({
  [ROUTE]: {
    accepts: { scheme: "exact", price: cfg.PRICE, network: cfg.X402_NETWORK,
               payTo: cfg.PAY_TO, maxTimeoutSeconds: 120 },
    serviceName: "BuildProof",
  },
}, resourceServer)
An unpaid request gets  with a  header. Decoded, the live one quotes scheme , network  (Base), amount  (USDC has 6 decimals, so $0.01), asset 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913. The buyer signs an EIP-3009 USDC authorization, a facilitator (PayAI, no API key) settles it, and the buyer retries with the payment header. The Worker never holds a private key and never sends a transaction. A malformed hash returns  before any price is quoted, and a failed chain read returns , which the middleware does not settle, so the buyer is not charged for my outage.On the  the marketplace itself is the A2A server and takes the payment ($0.01 USDC on NEAR). My Worker receives assignments by webhook, checks an HMAC-SHA256 over  with a 5-minute window, and a cron trigger polls every minute for missed ones. A brief without a hash is declined as , so the caller is not charged. On  the listing is a paid workflow in the public  catalogue; the platform calls the Worker as an external agent endpoint, with no LLM in the loop.Everything below runs without an account.git clone https://github.com/ttimesai-star/buildproof && cd buildproof && npm install

# A demo acceptance act registered on Base mainnet, two signers, one of them via a relayed EIP-712 signature
BP_RPC=https://mainnet.base.org node cli/buildproof.mjs verify examples/pdf/act_01.pdf
#   status ATTESTED, registered 2026-10-09T13:18:07Z, closed 2026-10-09T13:18:19Z, 2 signers signed

# The AI-output review manifest, co-signed by the AI operator and the human reviewer
BP_RPC=https://mainnet.base.org node cli/buildproof.mjs verify examples/legal/ai_review_manifest.json
#   status ATTESTED, type ai-output, registered 2026-10-10T00:45:03Z, closed 2026-10-10T00:45:13Z, 2 signers signed

# The x402 quote (expect HTTP 402 and a PAYMENT-REQUIRED header)
curl -i https://buildproof-x402.zbignevich.workers.dev/v1/attestation/$(node cli/buildproof.mjs hash examples/pdf/act_01.pdf)

# Free service info and OpenAPI spec
curl https://buildproof-x402.zbignevich.workers.dev/
curl https://buildproof-x402.zbignevich.workers.dev/openapi.json
The whole demo story (contract, amendment, a clean act, an act with planted errors that the client rejects with the hash of the check report) runs on a local  chain with . CI runs it, plus the Foundry, Python and Node suites, on every push; the latest commit is green.What it costs, what it is notDeploying the registry and running the mainnet demo cost 0.0000096 ETH in gas, about $0.024. Registering and co-signing the AI-output manifest (three transactions) cost 0.0000022 ETH, about half a cent. That is the economic argument for an L2: co-signing a document costs less than printing it.The limits, stated plainly: Every record on mainnet is demo data with synthetic parties. No paid x402 call has been made on mainnet so far. The AI-output manifest flow is covered by tests on a local chain, and the example manifest is registered and co-signed on mainnet, but both signers are my own demo wallets, not an independent operator and reviewer.A wallet signature is not a qualified electronic signature under eIDAS or ESIGN. BuildProof is an evidence layer next to whatever signature the law requires, not a replacement for it.Hashes of short documents can be guessed. A one-page template with a few variable fields can be brute-forced from a dictionary of likely values. A real contract with dates, bank details and amounts is a much harder target, but salted hashes for low-entropy forms are still on the roadmap, not in the code.The manifest names the model; it does not prove the model ran. It proves what was approved, by whom and when.The paid API is a single Worker. The data is decentralized; my convenience layer is not. Anyone can run  against the same registry.An Ethereum Attestation Service adapter, salted hashes for templates, and an agent-side helper that builds the manifest the moment the model answers, so pinning the output is a side effect of generating it. And the part that matters most: a first real counterparty who signs a real acceptance act this way. That decides whether this is a tool or a demo.The code, the tests and the draft of this article were produced by AI agents (mainly Claude by Anthropic, with a second-opinion review by Google's Jules agent), working on goals and constraints I set. The problem and the rules for checking acceptance acts come from my own work in construction. The agents proposed the architecture and wrote the code; every claim in this text was then verified against the repository and the live endpoints: the contract address and its Sourcify match, the mainnet attestation status via the CLI, the live 402 price quote, the test count, and the CI status. Where something has not been done, such as a paid mainnet call or real users, the article says so. The optional field extraction in the cross-checker can call an NVIDIA-hosted Nemotron model; in that path the model only extracts numbers, and deterministic Python decides every finding.

I built a 3D isometric office where each employee is a local AI agent running on a single RTX 4090. I assign a task, the agent plans, researches, drafts and self-checks, and I approve the result or send it back with a comment. The 3D scene was the easy part. The hard part was making a 27B model that shares one GPU with its colleagues stop inventing facts about me and do the work I asked for.What It Is (and What Inspired It)AI Office is a local web app. You open a browser and there is a 3D isometric office. Each figure at a desk is an AI agent. I assign a task, and the agent works through a fixed pipeline: plan, research, draft, self-check. I read the output, approve it, or send it back with a comment. The model runs on my machine; the only traffic that leaves it is web search.The idea came from WareTrack, a warehouse-management demo that Dilum Sanjaya posted on X in October 2026. WareTrack is a simulation. In AI Office the agents do real work: they call a language model, hit a search engine, and produce a document I can read.I built it for a simple reason: I delegate better when I can see the work happening. A chat window tells me a task is "in progress"; an office shows me who is working on it, who is waiting, and who has finished.The stack is deliberately small:- Next.js 15, React Three Fiber, Zustand (frontend)- FastAPI, PostgreSQL (backend)- Ollama running qwen3.8:27b (q4_K_M, 16K context window, set locally) on one RTX 4090, 24 GBEvery service runs on localhost. I built it in about three days across eight stages with Claude Code. At version 1.0 the repo sits at 70 commits, 270 backend tests, and 41 layout checks.The One Rule That Kept the 3D HonestPostgreSQL is the only source of truth. The 3D scene never decides where a figure stands or what it is doing. It renders.The data flows in one direction:1. PostgreSQL holds the state - which agent is at which desk, what task it is on, its status and when that status began.2. The FastAPI backend exposes that state through an API.3. A Zustand store in the browser reads from the API and holds the current snapshot.4. React Three Fiber reads from the store and draws the scene.Walking and driving animations are a pure function of state and time: position = f(state, t). Reload the page or open a second tab and every figure is in the same position, within a millimetre. The scene is a view, not a simulation.One GPU, One Queue, One Coffee MachineOne RTX 4090 means one inference request at a time. If two agents need the model, they queue. In practice, two agents alternate every 10 to 20 seconds.I gave the agents a kitchen. If an agent's wait for the model exceeds 30 seconds, it stands up, walks to the coffee machine, and waits there. The coffee queue is the GPU queue, made visible.A 30-second wait does not mean something is broken. It means more than one agent is ahead in the queue. The agent is not stuck; it is in line. The kitchen is just the queue rendered as a place you can see.Before I added instancing, the scene issued 893 draw calls for roughly 13k triangles. After switching to instanced meshes, draw calls dropped to 117 while the triangle count grew to 64k.Average CPU work per frame with six figures moving at once is about 1 ms at 165 Hz; p99 was 1.7 ms. I did not measure GPU time separately, so I will not pretend I have a full picture of the frame budget.On the inference side, the 27B model occupies about 17 GB of my 24 GB VRAM and produces roughly 100 tokens per second. A typical task finishes in about two minutes; with web research in the loop, three to five. Before committing to a model I benchmarked two candidates on the same card: qwen3.8:27b and muse-glimmer:30b. I chose qwen3.8:27b.One incident is worth describing. Speed fell from about 100 to 25–30 tokens per second with no code change. An orphaned model-server process was holding a second copy of the model in VRAM. It had been left behind when Ollama was force-stopped during a test. Killing it restored the baseline.What the Model Got Wrong (and What I Did About It)The first test runs produced three errors. The model wrote that Dumfries is "roughly 45 minutes from Glasgow", which is false. It mentioned a tool that was not in my profile. It described me as still completing a degree I already hold.The fixes were small and specific. I put today's date into the system prompt. I added a line-by-line self-check stage that runs before the result reaches me. And I added two house rules that go into the system prompt at every stage:Do not invent facts: no made-up experience, employers, projects, dates, numbers, names, links or quotes. Use only the director's profile, the task, the director's comments and earlier stage outputs.- Every statement about the director must be backed by a specific line of the profile, the task or the comments. Do not infer or estimate anything else: no distances or travel times, no availability, no tools, roles or responsibilities that are not listed, no skill levels. If a detail would help, mark it [NEEDS INPUT: ...].Each clause in the second rule is there because of a specific mistake.One invented phrase still got through the self-check. The phrase was "available to start immediately", which I had never said. Final review stays with me.The agent has two web tools: search, through a local SearXNG instance, and page fetch. Both are read-only. They can search, read the text of a public page and follow a redirect to another public address. They cannot log in or send cookies, submit forms or post anything, reach localhost or a private network address, or run JavaScript on the page.Before any page is fetched, the target address is checked after DNS resolution. If any resolved address is local or private, the request is refused:def is_public_ip(address: str) -> bool:
    ip = ipaddress.ip_address(address.split("%", 1)[0])
    if isinstance(ip, ipaddress.IPv6Address):
        mapped = ip.ipv4_mapped or ip.sixtofour or (ip.teredo[1] if ip.teredo else None)
        if mapped is not None:
            ip = mapped
    return ip.is_global and not ip.is_multicast and not ip.is_reserved


async def resolve_public(target: Target, resolver: Resolver = system_resolver) -> str:
    """Returns the IP to connect to; refuses when any resolved address is local or private."""
    try:
        ipaddress.ip_address(target.host.strip("[]"))
        addresses = [target.host.strip("[]")]
    except ValueError:
        try:
            addresses = await resolver(target.host, target.port)
        except OSError as err:
            raise BlockedUrl(f"cannot resolve '{target.host}'") from err
    if not addresses:
        raise BlockedUrl(f"cannot resolve '{target.host}'")
    for address in addresses:
        if not is_public_ip(address):
            raise BlockedUrl(f"'{target.host}' points to a local or private address")
    return addresses[0]
The fetcher then connects to the checked IP itself and sends the real host name in the Host header and in SNI, so the name cannot resolve to something else between the check and the request. Every redirect goes through the same check.Page text reaches the model as data wrapped in delimiters, not as instructions. That reduces the chance that a hostile page talks the model into doing something; it does not remove it.Job boards answered 403 to page reads, so job search moved to official job APIs.Restart, Resume and What Still Needs a HumanStage state lives in the database. If the backend restarts mid-task, the interrupted stage is marked as interrupted and the same stage starts again. The agent does not re-plan or re-research.What still needs me is a short list. I approve the final output. I catch what the self-check misses. I decide whether a task is actually done. I assign the next task.What Happened With a Real TaskThe first real task I gave an agent was a job search: junior software roles in Glasgow and Edinburgh, four searches through two job APIs. It came back with 34 vacancies and recommended none of them.Most were training courses advertised as jobs. The agent quoted the lines that gave them away, such as "the cost of the training is the responsibility of the learner". The rest were graduate schemes I had excluded, or engineering roles that had nothing to do with software. It also flagged one role from its own notes that it could not find in the job list, and asked me to confirm it before trusting it.An empty list was the correct answer. My search terms were too narrow, and the agent said so.The model is fast and sometimes wrong, so the last check is mine. The office makes it obvious where that check is needed.
